From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jrash <johnrash@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 11:39:52 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 08/15] efi loader: boot the disk the loader came from; grow
 the heap

Started from a USB stick on a laptop whose NVMe disk holds
an installed Haiku, the stick's EFI loader booted the NVMe's system, and
its boot menu didn't list the stick.

- platform_add_boot_device() added the firmware's disks in the
  firmware's order, which lists the built-in disk first, and the first
  Haiku volume found is booted. Now the disk whose device path starts
  the device path of what the firmware loaded the loader from (its
  ESP partition, or the El Torito image OVMF boots a hybrid anyboot
  image through) goes first, behind a network disk. A disk
  without a system still falls through to the next. Every disk is
  logged ("efi: disk 1: 808 MiB, 512-byte blocks, the boot loader's").
- The boot menu mounts the system package of each volume it lists. The
  1.5 MiB heap had about 770 KB in use when the menu started (mostly
  the boot volume's package), its 152 old states took about 50 KB, and
  it ran out on the stick's
  (packagefs.cpp:859: Out of memory). With BOOT_SUPPORT_HEAP_GROWTH (EFI
  only), malloc() adds another region of the default size when the heap
  runs out, up to 8. Other platforms keep a single region.

New test qemu/run-loader-disk-test.sh: an internal NVMe disk listed
first (raw BFS, the image's system package, 152 old states) and the
stick; the loader must boot the stick, the menu must list both, and
started from an ESP-only disk the loader boots the internal disk and
still lists the stick. 8 of 8 with the new loader; the old one fails.
The arm64 compile check covers heap.cpp and devices.cpp.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
